Fixture quarantine: excluded from build/test execution and the new CMake/Ninja build

These are not deferred tests that run anyway. The existing fixture selector refuses
them, CI selection withholds them, and Gradle excludes their consumer classes in
all modes. The existing selection manifest carries quarantined=true on each named
group. Make and CI build only admitted CMake targets.
Do not register these producers or consumers in the new CMake/Ninja graph.
Additional classes use quarantinedJunit; mixed-class full-Core methods use
quarantinedJunitTags. Ordered foreign-exception Make preparation stops immediately.
Windows PowerShell selects only the remaining portable and direct ABI checks.
Direct producer commands remain source material for isolated repair, not build
entry points. Scheduled jobs do not invoke quarantined producers.

Readmission requires useful semantic coverage, named file inputs, one producer
per output file, and successful incremental regeneration without clearing a
published output directory or reading a previous attempt. Keep private scratch
files separate. A producer may have several declared outputs; an output may not
have several producers. Exact compiler counts and historical receipts do not
justify preserving a fixture.

Previous attempts affect output names or are prerequisites:
  process-lifecycle Core acquisition (054): always extracts source-<timestamp>,
    rewrites its Cabal project and rebuilds process. The separate native process
    target remains admitted. Give the pinned archive, stable extraction and Cabal
    component their own file rules before restoring these two Core consumers.
  original-posix-stat group (075): current-directory always extracts a timestamped
    Unix source tree; directory-streams invokes that producer when its receipt is
    absent. Split useful independent ABI fixtures from this ten-producer chain;
    shared Unix acquisition needs one stable owner consumed by both directory tests.
  simd-int32x4-bytearray (030), simd-word32x4-bytearray (031),
  simd-floatx4-bytearray (032), simd-doublex2-bytearray (033): copy every old
    receipt artifact before generation; missing old files block replacement;
    random attempt directories become published inputs.
  boxed-cas (044), boxed-array-extensions (049), original-stack (098): select
    run-N using existing directories; move/archive previous manifests.

Unrelated directory members change consumed inputs or make generation fail:
  bytearray (018), mutable-bytearrays (019), resize-bytearrays (020),
  mutable-bytearray-size (021), compare-byte-arrays (022): consume every stage CBD.
  simd-address-families (027): manifest collects generated source/*.hs leftovers.
  data-to-tag (039), proxy-void (042): consume all CBDs found in the output tree.
  fetch-add-int-array (045), atomic-int-arrays (046), shrink-bytearrays (047),
    mutvar (081): exact output-directory inventories reject unrelated CBDs.
    show-word-list (127), short-bytes-slices (129), unsafe-equality (134),
    tag-to-enum (135): recursively discovered products enter receipts consumed
    by tests.
  stable-pointers (084): both original and shared-CAF exports require exact
    output-directory inventories. Unrelated CBDs must not prevent regeneration;
    replace both listings with their declared pairs before readmission.
  compiled-thunk-retention (097): floating preparation remains quarantined.
    The independent four-root boxed-array product is admitted as boxed-arrays;
    it consumes only its named CBD pair per stage.
  array-slices (123): consumes every stage CBD and also rewrites shared coverage.

Published directories are cleared as part of the recipe:
  address-identity (101): removes build/addr-identity.
  synchronous-exceptions (103): selection/default script removes its output tree;
    producer also enumerates stage CBDs.
  corpus (119): removes group output directories before export.
  managed-mvars (133): selected --refresh deletes the output tree.

Joint output ownership:
  cbv-coercion (014): independently produces tuple-arithmetic's pre CBD and an
    explicit64-primops CBD. The owning arithmetic producers must be the sole
    writers; a consumer needing those files must depend on their producers.
  mutable-bytearrays (019), resize-bytearrays (020), mutable-bytearray-size (021),
    array-slices (123): regenerate build/primop-coverage.json from inside recipes.
    Shared coverage must have one separate owning rule.
  backend-annotations (040): independently publishes build/thc-fixtures.path,
    also published by compact-model and common/default setup. The backend fixture
    must consume the encoder from one shared owner instead.
  simd-capability-smoke (116): invokes the generator that also writes application
    Java/fixture sources through Gradle. The generator needs one owner and the
    fixture must consume the generated sources, not regenerate them.

Shared infrastructure also excluded from translation as fixture rules:
  interface-core (085): runs InstalledCacheFixtures.checkInstalledCache during
    generation, destroys/recreates its cache, corrupts interfaces and sources, and
    publishes test-success booleans. A fixture cache hit would skip those tests.
    Package DB initialization also changes the published log set on repeat runs.
    Separate retained/thin interface products and native linkage artifacts from
    cache-correctness tests; run the latter as tests with private scratch state.
    Both pinned-source and complete retained-Core provider paths remain required.

Not quarantined merely for this reason:
  Test-private filesystem state used to check unlink/rename/directory semantics.
  A shared tool called by several rules, provided its products have ONE owning
    rule and callers only consume those products.
  The application runtime's native providers are Gradle-owned application products,
    not fixture admission decisions. Their incrementality defects are recorded in
    fixture-inputs.log's shared appendix and still need repair at their owner.

  aggregate-heap (145, custom full-Core): both producer and consumer enumerate
    stage/Core output directories. AggregateHeapNativeTest is excluded until named
    module outputs replace the hidden input set. Preserve useful heap layout and
    laziness behavior when consolidating aggregate tests.

  fourway-aggregate (146, custom full-Core): producer and native consumer discover
    stage CBDs by listing output directories. All three FourWayAggregate native,
    storage and protocol consumer classes are excluded until ownership is fixed.

  narrow-integer-transport (147, custom full-Core): both sides list stage CBDs.
    NarrowIntegerTransportTest is excluded. After repair this general corpus may
    replace smaller narrow fixtures; split SIMD so scalar coverage does not force LLVM.

  generic-sum-transport (148, custom full-Core): producer and consumer list stage
    CBDs. GenericSumTransportTest is excluded. Keep typed carrier/GC-lifetime
    boundaries and recover direct storage/rejection controls without native setup.

  graph-bfs (153): publishes wall-clock run directories and shares archive/tool acquisition.
    GraphWorkloadTest excluded. Retain useful package workload in intensive coverage
    after stable outputs/shared acquisition replace the bespoke preparation.

  original-iconv (154): uses unhashed installed Core/ambient locale and demands exact private
    foreign-call identity/counts. OriginalIconvNativeTest excluded; public encoding
    package behavior should replace this bespoke iconv harness.

  file-wait (155): consumer requires compiledEntries==before+1. FileWaitFullCoreTest excluded;
    retain readiness, bad-FD payload laziness and actual compiled execution with
    shared package acquisition.

  compact-regions and compact-serialization (158): both require auditor exit1 with exactly one Conc.Bound module-format issue
    before selecting originals. Both native consumers excluded; successful package
    discovery must be allowed, and original modules need explicit ownership.

  stm (159): discovery requires auditor failure with one exact Conc.Bound issue.
    STMFullCoreTest excluded. Preserve transaction/concurrency/resumption semantics;
    remove error-dependent acquisition and share package products.

  stableptr-ffi (161): preparation executes a preexisting installDist/THC_TEST_RUNTIME without a
    producer edge. StablePtrFfiFullCoreTest excluded until runtime/package products
    are explicit dependencies and execution is separated from preparation.

  foreign-exceptions (163): runtime-core output-directory members become consumed inputs. Full-Core
    foreign-exception tag and ordered Make producer lane excluded, including dependent
    interop/array methods. Preserve exception, Safe API and host-lifetime contracts.

  java-arrays (164): reads foreign-exception installed products by default without its own graph
    dependency. Full-Core tagged methods excluded; direct fixture-free Java array
    tests remain active.

  package-native-demand and package-scalar-cbits (165): both execute THC_TEST_RUNTIME/default installDist during preparation with
    no build edge. Scalar custom consumer and demand full-Core tag excluded; retain
    canonical state/ABI/isolation in one lean package integration case.

  package-native-originals (166): copied source inventory requires a fresh directory when files disappear;
    CBD staging enumerates outputs. PackageNativeOriginalsTest excluded. Replace
    standalone digest/erf/primitive scaffolding with public package integration.

  hashable-ffi (170): runs a prebuilt THC runtime during fixture preparation without an owning
    build edge. HashableFfiFullCoreTest excluded; keep public hashing behavior in
    intensive package integration using shared acquisition.

  package-native-archives (171): recursive capture-directory CBD enumeration supplies modules. Full-Core
    archive consumer excluded; retain native linker/ABI and deliberately invalid
    declaration controls with explicit outputs and shared acquisition.

  windows-codepages (172): publishes a new timestamped interfaces/providers/runtime-support tree each
    run. WindowsCodePagesTest and its PowerShell producer selection excluded; retain
    required platform ownership/error tests using normal package linkage.

  windows-directory (173): publishes fresh timestamped upstream source evidence and rebuilds a private
    Win32 package on every attempt. Directory consumer/PowerShell producer selection
    excluded; private filesystem cases themselves are legitimate runtime state.

  windows-smoke, bridge, driver and timeout (174): smoke writes general corpus/native/CString products; bridge/driver discover
    outputs and publish timestamped trees. WindowsDistributionTest and automated
    smoke/driver preparation excluded; standalone timeout diagnostic remains usable.

The current admission inventory is in .github/scripts/fast-fixtures.json.
Full-Core exception tags are also withheld. Selection counts do not establish
runtime pass/fail results; quarantine does not resolve the underlying regressions.
